102-    Runtime(String, Span),
103-    /// One of the host's own resource limits — the step budget, the call-depth
104-    /// limit, or a loop's iteration cap.
105-    ///
106-    /// A variant rather than a `Runtime` whose message happens to read like one,
107-    /// because the question `might fail` asks of a failure — *is this the host
108-    /// stopping the program, or the program's own expression going wrong?* — has
109-    /// to be answerable from the failure itself. Matching a message prefix made
110-    /// the answer depend on the wording: rewording a limit message would silently
111-    /// turn every guard around a runaway program into one that swallows it, and
112-    /// any failure a program produced that began the same way would be reported as
113-    /// a resource error and escape the guard written for it. It renders as a
114-    /// `RuntimeError` and reads exactly as one — a limit is a runtime failure — but
115-    /// nothing has to agree on how it is spelled for it to be told apart.
116-    Limit(String, Span),
117-    Io(String),
118-}
